## Executive Thesis

`s3-compat-replay` is strongest when positioned as a local, evidence-backed semantic
compatibility preflight for S3-compatible object-storage migrations and endpoint
changes. It is not a migration engine, benchmark, pricing model, or object copier.
Its business value is that it converts real observed object-storage behavior into a
targeted probe plan, runs safe synthetic checks against a target scratch bucket, and
then translates mismatches into a cutover decision brief, remediation queue, owner
questions, and migration risks before a production cutover.

The core business story is simple: object-copy success does not prove application
compatibility. Applications often depend on API semantics around CORS, presigned
URLs, version IDs, object tags, metadata round trips, multipart uploads, legal hold,
retention, delete markers, range reads, conditional requests, ACL behavior, ownership
controls, and list pagination. Those are exactly the areas this repo models, probes,
and turns into a business-facing cutover recommendation.

Important caveat: the fresh run used the repo's deterministic generator and simulated
semantic target results. That is appropriate for demo coverage and product validation,
but it is not a certification of any real endpoint. A live deployment must run the
generated probe plan against the customer's chosen target endpoint and scratch bucket.

## External Reference Frame

The analysis below is grounded in the repo and these external references:

- Amazon S3 API Reference:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/Type_API_Reference.html
  The S3 API surface contains bucket-level and object-level operations, data types,
  and behavior contracts. This supports the product thesis that compatibility is more
  than basic object upload and download.

- CloudTrail data events:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html
  CloudTrail data events include object-level API activity such as `GetObject`,
  `PutObject`, and `DeleteObject`. This supports the repo's use of CloudTrail-style
  events as observed usage evidence while also explaining why request hints and HTTP
  traces are needed for fields CloudTrail does not fully expose.

- S3 CloudTrail behavior:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-logging-s3-info.html
  The S3-specific CloudTrail documentation distinguishes bucket-level management
  events from object-level data events. This is directly relevant to the tool's input
  model and warnings about incomplete evidence.

- Server access logging:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html
  Server access logs provide detailed request records useful for security and access
  audits. This supports the repo's optional server-access-log input path.

- S3 data consistency:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html
  S3's documented consistency model matters because applications may assume immediate
  visibility of writes, deletes, tags, ACLs, metadata, and list results. The repo
  correctly treats consistency as a semantic behavior to review.

- Object Lock:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html
  Object Lock uses WORM-style controls with retention periods and legal holds, and is
  tied to versioned object behavior. This supports the repo's Object Lock and
  retention business stories.

- NIST SP 800-209, Security Guidelines for Storage Infrastructure:
  https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/209/final
  NIST frames storage as a major infrastructure pillar and highlights configuration
  control, data protection, isolation, restoration assurance, and encryption. The
  repo maps well to that risk-management framing.

- NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Contingency Planning Guide:
  https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/final
  Contingency and recovery planning are relevant because object-storage migrations
  often affect backup, archive, retention, recovery, and production restoration paths.

